  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "age-06-products-squares-quadratics",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Products, Squares, and Quadratic Age Relations",
    "description": "Use products and simple quadratic equations to determine ages, then reject values that cannot represent valid ages.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with concrete ages, labelled dates, separate calculation lines, a reason for each step, and an original-clue check. Place a fresh matching practice question after each worked example; wrong answers receive a worked correction and parallel practice.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Handle chapter-level age problems involving the product of two ages together with their sum, difference, or another linear relation. Build the equation from the stated relationship, use factor pairs or simple factoring to find candidate ages, and explain why two algebraic roots may appear while only one pair may satisfy the people’s age order and the problem's time conditions. Where a sum and product are known, connect them to a quadratic only at an elementary factoring level. Check candidates in the original product and linear condition; reject negative, fractional, or otherwise inadmissible values only when the stated context or integer-age assumption requires it. Do not teach general quadratic formula methods beyond the source level."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's find ages when the clues include multiplication.\n\nA sum tells us how much two ages make together. A product tells us what we get when we multiply them. Both clues can point to one pair of ages.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two ages add to 20 and multiply to 96. Think of whole-number factor pairs of 96.\n\n1 and 96 are too far apart. So are 2 and 48, 3 and 32, and 4 and 24.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The pair 8 and 12 works:\n8 + 12 = 20\n8 × 12 = 96.\n\nBoth clues must be true. A pair that fits the product alone is not enough. For larger values, an equation gives us the same test.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
