    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "We can find the sum of two squared ages without first finding each age. Suppose ages x and y add to 25 and multiply to 144.\n\nStart with the square of their sum.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5_step": {
      "id": "teach_5_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "(x + y)² = x² + 2xy + y².\n\nSo x² + y² = (x + y)² − 2xy\n= 25² − 2 × 144\n= 625 − 288 = 337.\n\nCheck with ages 16 and 9: 16 + 9 = 25, 16 × 9 = 144, and 16² + 9² = 337.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_5"
        }
      ]
    },
